  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "8L8Z5f4ZfYZsCo+RKoGfIp5W3kk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:02.324Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The Frank!Framework is Apache-2.0 licensed and, per the research, already runs more than 10,000 integrations in production. Its function is to move messages between systems. What it does not do is keep a business record of what each exchange decided, which is a different concern from whether the message was delivered successfully.\n\nThat is the gap in its strongest available form: the framework's own purpose excludes the record layer, so the missing piece is complementary rather than competitive. The research puts the seam at one adapter \u2014 the framework's existing extension mechanism \u2014 which bounds the integration cost and makes it legible to the people who maintain the code.\n\nLicence and installed base are what make this the strongest fit in this part of the corpus. Apache-2.0 means a contribution can be taken upstream without a licensing negotiation, and the existing deployments mean the record layer arrives wherever the framework already runs, so the value does not depend on winning clients one at a time.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- WeAreFrank! (NL) \u2014 Erwin Beets, Founder & Managing Partner \u00b7 info@wearefrank.nl \u00b7 https://wearefrank.nl\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> The Frank!Framework (Apache-2.0) already runs 10,000+ integrations in production \u2014 it moves messages between systems but keeps no business record of what each exchange decided. Powerhouse is the record layer their own framework lacks; the seam is one adapter.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:29:02.324688Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:02.324695Z"
    }
  },
